"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a pathological stage of pT2, negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, and nodal status is uncertain as no nodes were sampled, meaning pNx is not equivalent to pN0. The preoperative PSA was 2.11. The surgical findings are favorable, with the report stating, \"The robot-assisted radical prostatectomy specimen showed Gleason 3+3 (ISUP grade group 1), pathological stage pT2. There was no extraprostatic extension; surgical margins were negative; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was absent; no lymph nodes were removed.\"\n\nThe prognosis is influenced by the favorable surgical findings, including negative margins and no seminal vesicle invasion. However, the lack of nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The preoperative PSA and the subsequent postoperative PSA trajectory are critical factors that would revise this prognosis. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data currently limits definitive assessment. The heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence, but this is a sensitivity range, not a calibrated prediction. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is essential to refine the outlook. The fixed estimated horizon is 75.60 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 49.14–102.05 months; its coverage has not been validated."
